k='lem-dj-bridge-sign-automorphism-and-star-dual';after[k]['strategy']=r"""The quadratic relation gives $T_i^{-1}=q^{-1}(T_i-(q-1))$, so $\theta(T_i)=-qT_i^{-1}=(q-1)-T_i$. Inverses satisfy the braid and distant-commutation relations, so their uniform scalar multiples do too; the quadratic relation is preserved by interchanging its roots $q,-1$. Applying $\theta$ twice fixes every generator. Hence it is an involutive algebra automorphism, and it commutes with star because both constructions fix the reversed generator relations. On a reduced word, $\theta(T_w)=(-q)^{\ell(w)}T_{w^{-1}}^{-1}$. All these formulas are universal and survive base change.
For pullback, $\theta$ is onto, so a subspace or submodule is stable for the new action exactly when it was stable for the old action. The same underlying inclusion and quotient maps prove exactness, all submodule/radical/simple and composition-series claims, and twisting twice is identity. The action $(f\cdot h)(m)=f(mh^*)$ satisfies $((f\cdot h)\cdot k)(m)=f(mk^*h^*)=f(m(hk)^*)$. For finite free modules, the dual basis identifies base changes, and commuting $\theta$ with star proves $(M^\theta)^\circ=(M^\circ)^\theta$.
To compute $\theta(x_\lambda)$ without any generic semisimplicity, use its left $-1$ eigenvalue for each parabolic generator: $T_i\theta(x_\lambda)=\theta(((q-1)-T_i)x_\lambda)=-\theta(x_\lambda)$. If $a=\sum_{w\in S_\lambda}a_wT_w$ is such a common eigenvector, pairing $w,s_iw$ with $\ell(s_iw)=\ell(w)+1$ in the standard multiplication formula gives $qa_{s_iw}=-a_w$. Thus $a_w=(-q)^{-\ell(w)}a_1$ by a finite reduced word; the common eigenspace is the free line $Ry_\lambda$ over every ring, without dividing by $q+1$ or a quantum factorial. For the longest parabolic word $w_0$ of length $N$, expand each product $\theta(T_w)=\prod_j((q-1)-T_{i_j})$. Its unique top-length standard term is $(-1)^{\ell(w)}T_w$; any omitted letter has fewer factors and cannot create length $N$. Consequently the coefficient of $T_{w_0}$ in $\theta(x_\lambda)$ is $(-1)^N$. In $cy_\lambda$ it is $c(-q)^{-N}$, so $c=q^N$, using only this unit. Thus $\theta(x_\lambda)=q^Ny_\lambda$ and applying $\theta$ again gives $\theta(y_\lambda)=q^{-N}x_\lambda$. At $q=1$, $T_{w^{-1}}^{-1}=T_w$, so $\theta(T_w)=(-1)^{\ell(w)}T_w$: it is sign pullback, not inversion of the permutation. Empty and singleton cases have $N=0$. No choice principle is used."""
k='lem-dj-bridge-permutation-form-invariance-and-restriction';after[k]['strategy']=r'''The distinguished row basis is proved free, so the diagonal weights $q^{\ell(d)}$ define the stated symmetric bilinear form. For each generator $T_i$, the row tabloids split into singleton orbits (same row, action scalar $q$) and two-element swap orbits. Order a swap pair with the shorter representative first. Its right-action matrix is $A_i=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\q&q-1\end{pmatrix}$ and its Gram matrix is $D=\operatorname{diag}(q^a,q^{a+1})$. Direct multiplication gives $A_iD=DA_i^{\mathsf T}$; singleton blocks satisfy the same equality, and different blocks are orthogonal. Thus $\beta(xT_i,y)=\beta(x,yT_i)$ for all row-module vectors. Iteration over a generator word, reversing the word on the second side, gives $\beta(xh,y)=\beta(x,yh^*)$ for every $h$. Restriction to the actual submodule $P(\lambda)$ is contravariant. All coordinates and structure constants are universal Laurent polynomials, so the whole proof survives arbitrary base change. Nondegeneracy of the restricted form is not asserted and no choice is used.'''
k='lem-dj-bridge-theta-transport-of-the-cellular-structure';after[k]['statement']=after[k]['statement'].replace('$\\{\\theta(m_{st}):s,t\\in\\operatorname{Std}(\\lambda)\\}$ is again an $R$-basis of $H$', '$\\{\\theta(m^{\\mu}_{st}):\\mu\\vdash n,\\ s,t\\in\\operatorname{Std}(\\mu)\\}$ is again an $R$-basis of $H$');after[k]['strategy']=r'''Because $\theta$ is an involutive $R$-algebra automorphism commuting with star, its image of the FULL Murphy basis (all partition shapes) is again a basis, and it transports the cellular involution and coefficient-independent multiplication congruences with image ideals $\theta(I^{\unrhd\lambda})$ and $\theta(I^{\rhd\lambda})$. These image ideals are spans of the corresponding transported shapes and are two-sided; they need not equal the original ideals. For $n=2$, $\theta(1+T_1)=q-T_1$ is not in $A(1+T_1)$, by comparison of the two standard coefficients. The explicit basis formula follows by applying $\theta$ to all factors of $m_{st}=T_{d(s)}^*x_\lambda T_{d(t)}$, using $\theta(x_\lambda)=q^{\ell(w_0(S_\lambda))}y_\lambda$. On the domain $C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda)^\theta$, the map $x\mapsto\theta(x)$ obeys $\theta(x\cdot h)=\theta(x\theta(h))=\theta(x)h$ and hence is an actual module isomorphism to the transported cell row. Define its image form by $G_{\rm image}(\theta(x),\theta(y))=G(x,y)$. Associativity follows from $G(x\theta(h),y)=G(x,y\theta(h)^*)=G(x,y\theta(h^*))$, which is exactly the transported relation with ordinary target action. Symmetry and the image radical follow from the same invertible coordinate map. The form is the transported cellular multiplication coefficient since applying $\theta$ preserves that scalar. This separates domain pullback from target transported row, includes every shape, and uses no generic identification, choice or old convention. All identities survive base change.'''
